3378,"Re: how to uninstall java preview? (Trial Version)","On Apr 6, 2010, at 10:40 AM, Gabriele Kahlout wrote:

> Hello,
> 
> Today presenting my application to a macbook user with the latest java
> releasion (1.6.17)
> my application failed to install properly (see the java console) and
> error JOptionPane .
> 
> On my machine the same works flawlessly. However I've this preview
> installed (1.6.19) and so I'm failing to re-produce the same testing
> environment. So I'd like to go back to the version my users have to be
> able to better assist them, unless you have better suggestions.
> 
> I'd be very thankful if you could also report to me if others have
> problems, just launching this (you need to choose an installation
> directory, and after doing so the error should appear):
> https://sourceforge.net/projects/memorizeasy/

WARNING: THIS IS A VERY BAD THING TO DO, AND WILL PUT YOUR MACHINE INTO AN UNSUPPORTABLE CONFIGURATION!

There is no official way to roll back to the GM version, however the installer for 10.6 Update 1 is at <http://support.apple.com/kb/dl972>, and the 10.5 Update 6 is at <http://support.apple.com/kb/dl971>.

If you manually modify the bundle version in the /System/Library/Frameworks/JavaVM.framework/Resources/Info.plist, the installer should install on top of the developer preview, however it will not be aware of some of the new content in the DP. This causes some tools like Java VisualVM to fail, because it's bundle will contain a hodgepodge of GM and DP bits, and the GM installer doesn't know to remove the DP bits (because when it was created, the DP bits we not invented yet).

This should be enough to test the 1.6.0_17 JVM, but this is hardly a ""valid"" configuration. Installing the 10M3044/9M3144 DP again on top of this mixed mess _should_ clean up the GM bits and put you into a valid DP configuration. Probably. Most likely.

If things get too out of control, you should always be able to move aside the /System/Library/Frameworks/JavaVM.framework/Versions directory, and re-run either the GM or DP installer, and you should get a fully coherent Java installation (modulo the tools in /usr/share/java and a other few exceptions in /System/Library which don't really change between versions).

Best of luck,
Mike Swingler
Java Runtime Engineer
Apple Inc. _______________________________________________
